---
title: Palak Paneer
layout: recipe
categories:
- curry
region: South Asia
---

Heat @neutral oil{1/4%cup} in a large, wide-bottomed pan over medium-high heat. Add the @cumin seeds{1%tsp} and allow to sizzle and pop for about 30 seconds, and then the chopped @yellow onion{1%medium}. Saute them together for about 10 minutes, until the onions start to brown.

Since you're already using the food processor later, go ahead and use it for the fine chopping as well: pulse Add @garlic{5%cloves}, @ginger{1%inch}, and @green chili{1-2%small} with a bit of water. Add this to the onions: the water will help deglaze the bottom of the pan. Saute together until the raw smell fades, about 2 minutes. If the onions are still looking a little light, deglaze with a bit of water and saute a while longer.

Add the diced @tomatoes{2%ripe}, @coriander powder{1 1/2%tsp}, @cumin powder{1%tsp}, @turmeric powder{1/2%tsp}, @red chili powder{1/2-3/4%tsp}, @garam masala{1/2%tsp} and @kosher salt{1 1/2%tsp} and continue to sauté for 3-4 minutes, until the oil starts to separate. If needed, deglaze again with some water, though the liquid from the tomatoes should help the browning complete.

Increase the heat to high and add the @spinach{1%lb}, a handful at a time, and stir into the masala until visibly wilted: it usually takes me about 5 minutes to get through the whole container.

Reduce the heat to medium, cover, and cook for another 10 minutes, stirring once in between. Make sure to give everything a good stir beforehand to make sure there's nothing still sticking to the bottom of the pan.

Meanwhile, if you're pan-searing the @paneer{8%oz}, heat 1 1/2 tbsp neutral oil in a large nonstick pan over medium heat: cut the paneer into bite-size cubes, and put them in a single layer on the pan. Cook until golden brown on each side: turn down the heat if they start to sputter.

Uncover the spinach masala and stir to mix everything up again. Remove from heat and allow to cool for couple minutes before putting it in the #food processor{}. Blend to a rough purée. You don't want a completely homogeneous mixture, variety in texture is nice: but mix to your preference

Place the pan back over medium-high heat and add the @ghee{2%tbsp} (you can just use neutral oil again: I don't recommend butter because it's smoke point is too low and it will burn. If you still want the buttery flavor, melt the same amount of normal, unsalted butter into the mixture AFTER you add the rest of the liquids). Add the spinach mixture, and fry for about 3 minutes. Mix @water{1/2%cup} into the curry, ensuring everything is well incorporated.

Stir in the paneer and @heavy cream{1/2%cup}. Reduce the heat to medium-low, cover, and cook for about 10 minutes, until the paneer soaked up the flavorful sauce around it.

Crush the @(optional) kasuri methi{1/2%tbsp} between the palms of your hands and sprinkle it in. Taste and adjust salt, and stir in a bit of @lemon juice{1%tbsp}. Serve over rice or with naan bread or a similar staple grain
